QEMU changes the Windows port of libvirt needs (apply to QEMU's source tree
with: patch -p1 < qemu-for-libvirt-windows.patch).

- target/i386/whpx: WHPX initialisation crashed with -machine none, which
  libvirt uses to probe QEMU's capabilities.
- system/physmem.c: RAM discard on Windows, so that the memory a virtio
  balloon takes from the guest goes back to the host.
- Migration with WHPX (virsh save, restore, managedsave, dump, migrate):
  - accel/whpx: dirty page tracking (WHvMapGpaRangeFlagTrackDirtyPages,
    WHvQueryGpaRangeDirtyBitmap) for live migration, and no migration
    blocker; QEMU_WHPX_NO_DIRTY_TRACKING=1 turns the tracking off.
  - target/i386/whpx: the vCPU state a migration needs: XSAVE area of the
    size the hypervisor asks for, XSS, TSC_AUX, the Hyper-V MSRs of the
    guest (guest OS id, hypercall page, VP assist page, reference TSC page,
    SynIC), the partition's reference time; local APIC registers the
    hypervisor refuses (x2APIC LDR and DFR) are kept.  The Hyper-V
    synthetic timers, whose state this API cannot read, are no longer
    offered (guests use the local APIC timer): QEMU_WHPX_SYNTHETIC_TIMERS=1
    offers them again, with a migration blocker.
  - monitor/fds.c: get-win32-socket makes an overlapped socket, as socket()
    does.

Like QEMU, GPL-2.0-or-later.  Written with an AI assistant: QEMU's policy
(docs/devel/code-provenance.rst) forbids submitting it upstream.

